#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133619 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133810 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a57fe64c-23e2-57d1-b7fc-3543c69342f8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Roots of a Cubic Polynomial Given a Factor or a Root"}] :question/id #uuid "767b530b-c488-5560-8850-a8f2168648e9" :question/math-academy-id "e-5760" :question/problem "Let $q(x)=2x^3+3x^2-18x+8.$ Given that $q(2) = 0$, factor $q(x)$ completely and identify all three of its roots." :question/worked-solution "If $q(2) = 0$, then by the factor theorem $(x-2)$ is a factor of $q(x).$ To find the remaining zeros, we need to find the other factors of $q(x).$\n\n So $(x-2)$ is a factor of $q(x),$ we know that $(x-2)$ divides $q(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $q(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-2)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | $x^3$ | $x^2$ | $x^1$ | $x^0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\color{red}2$ | $2$ | $3$ | $-18$ | $8$ |\n|  | $\\color{gray}\\downarrow$ | $4$ | $14$ | $-8$ |\n|  | $2$ | $7$ | $-4$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x^3+3x^2-18x+8}{x-2} = 2x^2+7x-4,\n$$\n\nwhich means\n\n$$\n2x^3+3x^2-18x+8 = (x-2)(2x^2+7x-4).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\n2x^3+3x^2-18x+8 = (x-2)(2x-1)(x+4).\n$$\n\nWe can now equate each factor to $0$ and solve for $x$ to find the three roots:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+4 & =0\\, & 2x-1 & =0\\, & x-2 & =0 \\\\ x & =-4\\, & x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\, & x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133620 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133811 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "299354d9-f716-512e-b682-d82b97d03775" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Cubic Polynomial Given a Factor or a Root"}] :question/id #uuid "811e52fd-0786-5363-ae33-aeeb1378046b" :question/math-academy-id "e-5763" :question/problem "Given that $(x+2)$ is a factor of the polynomial $f(x)=x^3+2x^2-x-2,$ factor $f(x)$ completely." :question/worked-solution "To find the remaining roots, we need to find the other factors of $f(x).$\n\nBecause $(x+2)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ we know that $(x+2)$ divides $f(x).$ So, to begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x+2)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | $x^3$ | $x^2$ | $x^1$ | $x^0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\color{red}-2$ | $1$ | $2$ | $-1$ | $-2$ |\n|  | $\\color{gray}\\downarrow$ | $-2$ | $0$ | $2$ |\n|  | $1$ | $0$ | $-1$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^3+2x^2-x-2}{x+2} = x^2-1,\n$$\n\nwhich means\n\n$$\nx^3+2x^2-x-2 = (x+2) (x^2-1).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\nx^3+2x^2-x-2 = (x+2) (x+1)(x-1).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133640 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133825 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a3c42bac-2253-5ceb-9b1f-45adfc8a61e9" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Cubic Polynomial With Only One Real Factor"}] :question/id #uuid "194dad70-c5fe-597c-a2a8-bcec1d699595" :question/math-academy-id "e-6205" :question/problem "Given that $(x+2)$ is a factor of the polynomial $f(x) = x^3 - 4x^2 - 2x + 20,$ factor $f(x)$ over the real numbers." :question/worked-solution "To factor $f(x)$ we need to find its other factors.\n\nBecause $(x+2)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ we know that $(x+2)$ divides $f(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x+2)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | $x^3$ | $x^2$ | $x^1$ | $x^0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\color{red}-2$ | $1$ | $-4$ | $-2$ | $20$ |\n|  | $\\color{gray}\\downarrow$ | $-2$ | $12$ | $-20$ |\n|  | $1$ | $-6$ | $10$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^3 - 4x^2 - 2x + 20}{x+2} = x^2 - 6x + 10,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nx^3 - 4x^2 - 2x + 20 = (x+2)(x^2 - 6x + 10).\n$$\n\nTo see whether the quadratic polynomial $x^2 - 6x + 10$ can be factored any further, we calculate its discriminant:\n\n$$\nb^2 - 4ac = (-6)^2 - 4(1)(10) = -4 <           0\n$$\n\nSo, the quadratic polynomial $x^2 - 6x + 10$ cannot be factored over the real numbers since its discriminant is negative. \n\nTherefore, the full factorization of $f(x)$ over the real numbers is given by \n\n$$\nx^3 - 4x^2 - 2x + 20 = (x+2)(x^2 - 6x + 10).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133641 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133826 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a7eb3f33-1b17-5b5e-b196-2abab5504f97" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Cubic Polynomial That Factors Over the Reals But Not the Rationals"}] :question/id #uuid "e119ff72-d5a1-57da-90a1-60cc9d15ee87" :question/math-academy-id "e-6206" :question/problem "Given that $(x+1)$ is a factor of the polynomial $f(x) = x^3 - x^2 - 4x - 2,$ factor $f(x)$ over the rational numbers." :question/worked-solution "To factor $f(x)$ we need to find its other factors.\n\nBecause $(x+1)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ we know that $(x+1)$ divides $f(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x+1)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | $x^3$ | $x^2$ | $x^1$ | $x^0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\color{red}-1$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-4$ | $-2$ |\n|  | $\\color{gray}\\downarrow$ | $-1$ | $2$ | $2$ |\n|  | $1$ | $-2$ | $-2$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^3 - x^2 - 4x - 2}{x+1} = x^2 - 2x - 2,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nx^3 - x^2 - 4x - 2 = (x+1) (x^2 - 2x -2).\n$$\n\nTo see whether the quadratic polynomial $x^2 - 2x - 2$ can be factored any further, we calculate its discriminant:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal D = b^2 - 4ac = (-2)^2 - 4(1)(-2) = 12 >     0\n$$\n\nSo, the quadratic polynomial $x^2 - 2x - 2$ cannot be factored over the rational numbers since its discriminant is not a perfect square.\n\nTherefore, the full factorization of $f(x)$ (over the rational numbers) is given by \n\n$$\nx^3 - x^2 - 4x - 2 = (x+1)(x^2 - 2x - 2).\n$$\n\n**Important Note**: Although the quadratic polynomial $(x^2 - 2x - 2)$ does not factor over the rational numbers, it *does* have real roots, because $\\mathcal D > 0.$ Therefore, if we were asked to find all of the real roots of $f(x),$ we would need to solve the equations \n\n$$\nx+1 = 0\\qquad \\textrm{and}\\qquad x^2 - 2x - 2 = 0.\n$$\n\nThe quadratic equation could be solved using the quadratic formula or by completing the square."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552456 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133825 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a3c42bac-2253-5ceb-9b1f-45adfc8a61e9" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Cubic Polynomial With Only One Real Factor"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42b73b43-bab7-5fae-a1ef-19f4f1d150f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+3)(x+2)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551824} {:answer/id #uuid "4735c8d5-e5a1-529e-a19b-982bc4e8fc4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+3)^{2}(x-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551840} {:answer/id #uuid "6c1604d6-113e-5ce0-896d-2e678b82cfc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+3)({x}^{2}+x+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551917} {:answer/id #uuid "702a6e5d-9186-5cb6-8ef2-32793f005208" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+3)({x}^{2}+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551930} {:answer/id #uuid "7ab0ba9b-d658-5512-b253-dfbfb26da1b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+3)({x}^{2}+3x+6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551956}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6c1604d6-113e-5ce0-896d-2e678b82cfc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+3)({x}^{2}+x+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551917} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ed4a96f-5c56-5050-b4df-80ab876141dc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552308}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "73e5f0e5-34ff-58f3-baa3-f951a9265d77" :question/math-academy-id "q-112540" :question/problem "Given that $(x+3)$ is a factor of the polynomial $f(x)={x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}+6x+9,$ factor $f(x)$ over the real numbers." :question/worked-solution "To factor $f(x)$ we need to find its other factors.\n\nBecause $(x+3)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ we know that $(x+3)$ divides $f(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x+3)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-3$ | $1$ | $4$ | $6$ | $9$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-3$ | $-3$ | $-9$ |\n|  | $1$ | $1$ | $3$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}+6x+9}{x+3}={x}^{2}+x+3,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}+6x+9=(x+3)({x}^{2}+x+3).\n$$\n\nTo see whether the quadratic polynomial ${x}^{2}+x+3$ can be factored any further, we calculate its discriminant:\n\n$$\n{b}^{2}-4ac={1}^{2}-4(1)(3)=-11<0\n$$\n\nSo, the quadratic polynomial ${x}^{2}+x+3$ cannot be factored over the real numbers since its discriminant is negative. \n\nTherefore, the full factorization of $f(x)$ over the real numbers is given by \n\n$$\n{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}+6x+9=(x+3)({x}^{2}+x+3).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552474 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133826 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a7eb3f33-1b17-5b5e-b196-2abab5504f97" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Cubic Polynomial That Factors Over the Reals But Not the Rationals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c63d166-fd9b-50bc-a537-1f588fd8117e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-4)({x}^{2}-5x+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551696} {:answer/id #uuid "6d81aa85-bc0c-55b0-93f5-77a990e53802" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-4)({x}^{2}-3x-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551923} {:answer/id #uuid "c20e354e-cc9b-59e3-bc4e-48f1e36d848d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-4)({x}^{2}-x+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552129} {:answer/id #uuid "c7a7fcbd-1afe-5941-9d53-74d7bdd953a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-4)({x}^{2}-3x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552142} {:answer/id #uuid "d37d014b-e71a-5881-8734-fdcce552d0b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-4)({x}^{2}+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552166}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c7a7fcbd-1afe-5941-9d53-74d7bdd953a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-4)({x}^{2}-3x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552142} :answer-field/id #uuid "35c415ab-7402-506b-8355-cca2e800a1fa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552281}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8fccdf12-2a25-54ff-895d-1b990f2006e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-112551" :question/problem "Given that $(x-4)$ is a factor of the polynomial $f(x)={x}^{3}-7{x}^{2}+13x-4,$ factor $f(x)$ over the rational numbers." :question/worked-solution "To factor $f(x)$ we need to find its other factors.\n\nBecause $(x-4)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ we know that $(x-4)$ divides $f(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-4)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $1$ | $-7$ | $13$ | $-4$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $4$ | $-12$ | $4$ |\n|  | $1$ | $-3$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{3}-7{x}^{2}+13x-4}{x-4}={x}^{2}-3x+1,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n{x}^{3}-7{x}^{2}+13x-4=(x-4)({x}^{2}-3x+1).\n$$\n\nTo see whether the quadratic polynomial ${x}^{2}-3x+1$ can be factored any further, we calculate its discriminant:\n\n$$\n{b}^{2}-4ac=(-3)^{2}-4(1)(1)=5>0\n$$\n\nSo, the quadratic polynomial ${x}^{2}-3x+1$ cannot be factored over the rational numbers since its discriminant is not a perfect square.\n\nTherefore, the full factorization of $f(x)$ (over the rational numbers) is given by \n\n$$\n{x}^{3}-7{x}^{2}+13x-4=(x-4)({x}^{2}-3x+1).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552413 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133810 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a57fe64c-23e2-57d1-b7fc-3543c69342f8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Roots of a Cubic Polynomial Given a Factor or a Root"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27cef22d-d1f2-5fe9-89c7-fec27cc8016a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551754} {:answer/id #uuid "4055e129-d673-5b95-878d-b68dc6ba0991" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551820} {:answer/id #uuid "afce600f-a21c-5cf3-8072-ddf5adacaf95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552089} {:answer/id #uuid "bd6f2a3c-c87b-5130-b3dc-e9297e9dc757" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552117} {:answer/id #uuid "e432d47f-29b2-5ecd-9c84-4035911ca7e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552194}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e432d47f-29b2-5ecd-9c84-4035911ca7e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552194} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4776dc4-83d1-5d85-abfe-a8e8c4de2f26" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552397}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1adb95f2-1de2-5cd2-b372-b57ff2f582da" :question/math-academy-id "q-18153" :question/problem "A polynomial $f(x)$ is defined as $f(x)=2{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-25x+12.$ Given that $f(3)=0$, what is the sum of the roots of the polynomial?" :question/worked-solution "If $f(3)=0$, then by the factor theorem $(x-3)$ is a factor of $f(x).$ To find the other roots of the polynomial $f(x)$, we need to find the other factors of $f(x).$\n\nSince $(x-3)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ we know that $(x-3)$ divides $f(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-3)$ using synthetic division:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\enclose{bottom right}{3} & 2 & 1 & -25 & 12 \\\\  &  & 6 & 21 & -12 \\\\  & 2 & 7 & -4 & \\boxed{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{2{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-25x+12}{x-3}=2{x}^{2}+7x-4,\n$$\n\nwhich means\n\n$$\n2{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-25x+12=(x-3)(2{x}^{2}+7x-4).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\n(x-3)(2{x}^{2}+7x-4)=(x-3)(2x-1)(x+4).\n$$\n\nNow that our polynomial is fully factored, we can see that the three roots of the polynomial are\n\n$$\nx=3,\\,x=\\frac{1}{2},\\,x=-4.\n$$\n\nFinally, the sum of the roots is\n\n$$\n3+\\frac{1}{2}-4=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552434 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133810 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a57fe64c-23e2-57d1-b7fc-3543c69342f8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Roots of a Cubic Polynomial Given a Factor or a Root"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19a9278a-907c-5566-9b90-6cc51775b72e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551720}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "19a9278a-907c-5566-9b90-6cc51775b72e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551720} :answer-field/id #uuid "67ad6537-44fe-5864-86bd-286b3e4cfaa9" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552313} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "87a5979f-065d-5de4-b81d-3da117d6c7ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551994}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "87a5979f-065d-5de4-b81d-3da117d6c7ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551994} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac1ed121-2f62-5bc3-8d2c-cfecabddf518" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552355} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9e2d0b93-b49a-51ee-aaf9-05eed6dd600e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552047}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9e2d0b93-b49a-51ee-aaf9-05eed6dd600e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552047} :answer-field/id #uuid "d474e02a-ccc4-55e9-9c0d-b67ae3a35845" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552376}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "47c5c0f9-0372-541a-ab67-517cecd45015" :question/math-academy-id "q-269983" :question/problem "A polynomial $f(x)$ is defined as $f(x)={x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-10x+8.$ Given that $f(1)=0$, the roots of the polynomial $f(x),$ arranged in **ascending order**, are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}},$\\,x=$\n{{field-2}},$\\,$ and\n$\\,x=$\n{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "If $f(1)=0$, then by the factor theorem $(x-1)$ is a factor of $f(x).$ To find the other roots of the polynomial $f(x)$, we need to find the other factors of $f(x).$\n\nSince $(x-1)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ we know that $(x-1)$ divides $f(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-1)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $-10$ | $8$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $1$ | $2$ | $-8$ |\n|  | $1$ | $2$ | $-8$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-10x+8}{x-1}={x}^{2}+2x-8,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-10x+8=(x-1)({x}^{2}+2x-8).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\n(x-1)({x}^{2}+2x-8)=(x-1)(x-2)(x+4).\n$$\n\nNow that our polynomial is fully factored, we can see that the three roots of the polynomial, arranged in ascending order, are\n\n$$\nx=-4,\\,x=1,\\,x=2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552423 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133810 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a57fe64c-23e2-57d1-b7fc-3543c69342f8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Roots of a Cubic Polynomial Given a Factor or a Root"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c25d3761-714b-5735-8731-3977dd0ae72b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552130}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c25d3761-714b-5735-8731-3977dd0ae72b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552130} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ba28e96-2468-571c-a90f-8599bcd89474" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552324} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d0d5beb-e94d-5ba2-b87b-1cda9a9afafc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551771}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2d0d5beb-e94d-5ba2-b87b-1cda9a9afafc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551771} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb327591-f395-51fb-9504-ee10ccdebdef" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552363} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2fbc335e-8f27-5eee-bce2-76d4cb1a878b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551780}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2fbc335e-8f27-5eee-bce2-76d4cb1a878b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551780} :answer-field/id #uuid "d5618691-6ef8-50af-9b1f-e9e6c1102dc1" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552377}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "26aef9f4-359f-5b52-aa4f-51a7c5f79b8e" :question/math-academy-id "q-326315" :question/problem "A polynomial $g(x)$ is defined as $g(x)={x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}-x+4.$ Given that $g(4)=0$, the roots of the polynomial $g(x),$ arranged in **ascending order**, are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}},$\\,x=$\n{{field-2}},$\\,$ and\n$\\,x=$\n{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "If $g(4)=0$, then by the factor theorem $(x-4)$ is a factor of $g(x).$ To find the other roots of the polynomial $g(x)$, we need to find the other factors of $g(x).$\n\nSince $(x-4)$ is a factor of $g(x),$ we know that $(x-4)$ divides $g(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $g(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-4)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $1$ | $-4$ | $-1$ | $4$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $4$ | $0$ | $-4$ |\n|  | $1$ | $0$ | $-1$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}-x+4}{x-4}={x}^{2}-1,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}-x+4=(x-4)({x}^{2}-1).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\n(x-4)({x}^{2}-1)=(x-4)(x-1)(x+1).\n$$\n\nNow that our polynomial is fully factored, we can see that the three roots of the polynomial, arranged in ascending order, are\n\n$$\nx=-1,\\,x=1,\\text{ and}\\,x=4.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552490 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133825 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a3c42bac-2253-5ceb-9b1f-45adfc8a61e9" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Cubic Polynomial With Only One Real Factor"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5f20a00c-98ca-54a5-8acd-444c10231e0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^2-2x+8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551894}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5f20a00c-98ca-54a5-8acd-444c10231e0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^2-2x+8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551894} :answer-field/id #uuid "3adfded7-965a-5d56-83c6-9073e71d9c77" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552286}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ad3c509d-b392-55c9-8bbd-386b08676ca2" :question/math-academy-id "q-327191" :question/problem "Given that $(x+1)$ is a factor of the polynomial $f(x)={x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+6x+8,$ factor $f(x)$ over the real numbers.\n\n$f(x)=(x+1)\\cdot ($ \n{{field-1}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "To factor $f(x)$ we need to find its other factors.\n\nBecause $(x+1)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ we know that $(x+1)$ divides $f(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x+1)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $6$ | $8$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-1$ | $2$ | $-8$ |\n|  | $1$ | $-2$ | $8$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+6x+8}{x+1}={x}^{2}-2x+8,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+6x+8=(x+1)({x}^{2}-2x+8).\n$$\n\nTo see whether the quadratic polynomial ${x}^{2}-2x+8$ can be factored any further, we calculate its discriminant:\n\n$$\n{b}^{2}-4ac={2}^{2}-4(1)(8)=-28<0.\n$$\n\nSo the quadratic polynomial ${x}^{2}-2x+8$ cannot be factored over the real numbers since its discriminant is negative.\n\nTherefore, the full factorization of $f(x)$ over the real numbers is given by \n\n$$\n{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+6x+8=(x+1)\\cdot ({x}^{2}-2x+8).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552424 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133810 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a57fe64c-23e2-57d1-b7fc-3543c69342f8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Roots of a Cubic Polynomial Given a Factor or a Root"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "337b747e-f35b-5e4c-b856-7ee928473b4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551787} {:answer/id #uuid "cb0fa6ae-afd3-54b5-9a4e-d0c38f3a43f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552150} {:answer/id #uuid "d2bc8467-8042-5e15-bbe7-eb8d9e33cb3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552164} {:answer/id #uuid "e187c3b6-31b3-52e2-af8b-587e7baea1ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552186} {:answer/id #uuid "f6bc5abc-0da1-5e7b-8ede-21533acf2b43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552242}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cb0fa6ae-afd3-54b5-9a4e-d0c38f3a43f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552150} :answer-field/id #uuid "0255dbbb-dbb3-588c-97c7-af1c5b11d4cc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552257}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "349d9aca-faa9-56e3-8806-6dcea184619f" :question/math-academy-id "q-39370" :question/problem "A polynomial $f(x)$ is defined as $f(x)=4{x}^{3}-12{x}^{2}-x+3.$ Given that $f(3)=0$, what is the sum of the roots of the polynomial?" :question/worked-solution "If $f(3)=0$, then by the factor theorem $(x-3)$ is a factor of $f(x).$ To find the other roots of the polynomial $f(x)$, we need to find the other factors of $f(x).$\n\nSince $(x-3)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ we know that $(x-3)$ divides $f(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-3)$ using synthetic division:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\enclose{bottom right}{3} & 4 & -12 & -1 & 3 \\\\  &  & 12 & 0 & -3 \\\\  & 4 & 0 & -1 & \\boxed{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{4{x}^{3}-12{x}^{2}-x+3}{x-3}=4{x}^{2}-1,\n$$\n\nwhich means\n\n$$\n4{x}^{3}-12{x}^{2}-x+3=(x-3)(4{x}^{2}-1).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\n(x-3)(4{x}^{2}-1)=(x-3)(2x-1)(2x+1).\n$$\n\nNow that our polynomial is fully factored, we can see that the three roots of the polynomial are\n\n$$\nx=3,\\,x=\\frac{1}{2},\\,x=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the sum of the roots is\n\n$$\n3+\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}=3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552512 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133811 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "299354d9-f716-512e-b682-d82b97d03775" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Cubic Polynomial Given a Factor or a Root"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d108dcd-a550-5a45-a26b-ff0fb91bb567" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-1)(3x-1)(3x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551727} {:answer/id #uuid "1fa48219-3ac0-5a6e-a68c-8b2ef6b14ba8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-1)(x+1)(9x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551732} {:answer/id #uuid "ac19c399-7614-5413-983a-dcda5325a68b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-1)(3x-1)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552077} {:answer/id #uuid "c6f1b7e4-6df2-5875-9c05-031b2a93c09b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-1)^{2}(9x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552139} {:answer/id #uuid "ec53fd5a-19a2-5165-812c-86b52e5bf069" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-1)^{2}(9x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552217}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac19c399-7614-5413-983a-dcda5325a68b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-1)(3x-1)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552077} :answer-field/id #uuid "e80bed56-d839-5172-b2e4-e427d055adaa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552388}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ddb92873-9866-58c6-b7b0-d5953b026f54" :question/math-academy-id "q-40077" :question/problem "A polynomial $f(x)$ is defined as $f(x)=9{x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}+7x-1.$ Given that $f(1)=0$, factor $f(x)$ completely." :question/worked-solution "If $f(1)=0$, then by the factor theorem $(x-1)$ is a factor of $f(x).$ To factor $f(x)$ we need to find its other factors.\n\nSince $(x-1)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ we know that $(x-1)$ divides $f(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-1)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $9$ | $-15$ | $7$ | $-1$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $9$ | $-6$ | $1$ |\n|  | $9$ | $-6$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{9{x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}+7x-1}{x-1}=9{x}^{2}-6x+1,\n$$\n\nwhich means\n\n$$\n9{x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}+7x-1=(x-1)(9{x}^{2}-6x+1).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\n(x-1)(9{x}^{2}-6x+1)=(x-1)(3x-1)^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552494 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133826 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a7eb3f33-1b17-5b5e-b196-2abab5504f97" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Cubic Polynomial That Factors Over the Reals But Not the Rationals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b775d85-16fa-593b-af15-bafac47c5129" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-2)({x}^{2}-x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551765} {:answer/id #uuid "3a91ed08-0c2a-5fe2-ad5f-bbf5247a636c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-2)({x}^{2}-5x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551806} {:answer/id #uuid "4e20104e-bf8c-5156-8698-a87d7d71bf18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-2)(x-1)(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551852} {:answer/id #uuid "5b691eb8-ff20-59f1-9cf6-8943a9ad1253" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-2)({x}^{2}-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551883} {:answer/id #uuid "7611395f-0849-5b16-9965-3b74dc25e799" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-2)({x}^{2}-3x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551944}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7611395f-0849-5b16-9965-3b74dc25e799" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-2)({x}^{2}-3x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551944} :answer-field/id #uuid "8984f316-d032-5dd7-9375-69b44b8ffa66" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552333}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b5caf723-bcce-5d2b-851d-852d72f2df0c" :question/math-academy-id "q-73990" :question/problem "Given that $(x-2)$ is a factor of the polynomial $f(x)={x}^{3}-5{x}^{2}+5x+2,$ factor $f(x)$ over the rational numbers." :question/worked-solution "To factor $f(x)$ we need to find its other factors.\n\nBecause $(x-2)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ we know that $(x-2)$ divides $f(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-2)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $-5$ | $5$ | $2$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $2$ | $-6$ | $-2$ |\n|  | $1$ | $-3$ | $-1$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{3}-5{x}^{2}+5x+2}{x-2}={x}^{2}-3x-1,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n{x}^{3}-5{x}^{2}+5x+2=(x-2)({x}^{2}-3x-1).\n$$\n\nTo see whether the quadratic polynomial ${x}^{2}-3x-1$ can be factored any further, we calculate its discriminant:\n\n$$\n{b}^{2}-4ac=(-3)^{2}-4(1)(-1)=13>0\n$$\n\nSo, the quadratic polynomial ${x}^{2}-3x-1$ cannot be factored over the rational numbers since its discriminant is not a perfect square.\n\nTherefore, the full factorization of $f(x)$ (over the rational numbers) is given by \n\n$$\n{x}^{3}-5{x}^{2}+5x+2=(x-2)({x}^{2}-3x-1).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552409 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133811 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "299354d9-f716-512e-b682-d82b97d03775" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Cubic Polynomial Given a Factor or a Root"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02fc7821-6dd2-5096-aa2d-b83194fb5c5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2x-3)(x-2)(x-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551670} {:answer/id #uuid "7c6dccd7-db3b-51e7-ab06-afca42b7a546" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2x+3)(x-1)(x-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551962} {:answer/id #uuid "a3194eae-8f0e-5f7b-9733-624c92d6d388" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+2)(2x+3)(x-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552059} {:answer/id #uuid "bee8efbd-a9fd-51f2-9072-f8d98a6caa37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2x-1)(x-4)(x-6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552121} {:answer/id #uuid "bf4ba886-9a18-595a-b811-3460cdc3c322" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+2)(2x+1)(x-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552122}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "02fc7821-6dd2-5096-aa2d-b83194fb5c5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2x-3)(x-2)(x-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551670} :answer-field/id #uuid "0ff95e5b-84fb-5fe5-8cb4-3ab8cd0055c1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552267}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "148f1fdf-ffa7-5ddd-8683-94b6614f6d0f" :question/math-academy-id "q-7661" :question/problem "A polynomial $f(x)$ is defined as $f(x)=2{x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}+34x-24.$ Given that $f(4)=0$, factor $f(x)$ completely." :question/worked-solution "If $f(4)=0$, then by the factor theorem $(x-4)$ is a factor of $f(x).$ To factor $f(x)$ we need to find its other factors.\n\nSince $(x-4)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ we know that $(x-4)$ divides $f(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-4)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $2$ | $-15$ | $34$ | $-24$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $8$ | $-28$ | $24$ |\n|  | $2$ | $-7$ | $6$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{2{x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}+34x-24}{x-4}=2{x}^{2}-7x+6,\n$$\n\nwhich means\n\n$$\n2{x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}+34x-24=(x-4)(2{x}^{2}-7x+6).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-4)(2{x}^{2}-7x+6) & =(x-4)(2x-3)(x-2) \\\\  & =(2x-3)(x-2)(x-4).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
